<!-- SECTION 6: RECOMMENDATIONS -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="section" id="recommendations">
  <h2 class="section-title">6. Key Findings and Policy Recommendations</h2>
  <p class="section-desc">Synthesizing data-driven insights into actionable guidance for education policy analysts and school administrators.</p>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>Summary of Key Findings</h3>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Finding</th><th>Evidence</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1</td><td><strong>The SSC-to-HSC transition is the critical loss point.</strong></td><td>Students gain +0.262 GPA from Previous to SSC but lose −0.244 from SSC to HSC. Net progression is nearly zero (+0.018).</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2</td><td><strong>Socio-economic factors show weak associations with HSC outcomes.</strong></td><td>Income–HSC r = −0.008; parent education gap is only 0.086 GPA points; internet/tuition effects are &lt;0.02 GPA.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3</td><td><strong>Study quantity does not predict performance.</strong></td><td>Study hours–HSC r = −0.022; attendance–HSC r = −0.014. Students with fewer study hours slightly outperform heavy studiers.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>4</td><td><strong>Regional disparities are the largest observed effect.</strong></td><td>Barisal-Rangpur gap = 0.204 GPA; excellent rate ranges from 20.3% to 36.8% across districts.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>5</td><td><strong>Internet access can be counterproductive without support.</strong></td><td>In lowest-income homes, internet correlates with −0.106 GPA; in highest-income, +0.121.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>6</td><td><strong>Public-private effectiveness varies by district.</strong></td><td>Dhaka public leads by +0.234; Rajshahi private leads by +0.173. No uniform pattern.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="rec-card">
    <span class="rec-tag">High Priority</span>
    <h4>1. Target the SSC-to-HSC Transition with Bridge Programs</h4>
    <p>The data shows consistent GPA decline from SSC to HSC (−0.244). Invest in transition support programs — academic orientation, study strategy workshops, and mentorship — between the SSC and HSC stages. Districts like Rangpur (GPA Δ = −0.057) should receive these interventions first.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="rec-card">
    <span class="rec-tag">High Priority</span>
    <h4>2. Focus Regional Investment on Rangpur and Khulna</h4>
    <p>Rangpur has the lowest mean HSC (3.909), the lowest excellent rate (20.3%), and the most negative GPA progression (−0.057). Khulna follows with 3.946 mean HSC. These districts should be prioritized for teacher training, curriculum support, and infrastructure investment. Rajshahi's model (highest GPA Δ at +0.078) could serve as a template.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="rec-card">
    <span class="rec-tag">Medium Priority</span>
    <h4>3. Pair Internet Access Programs with Digital Literacy Support</h4>
    <p>Unguided internet access shows a negative association with performance in lower-income families (−0.106 GPA in Q1). Connectivity initiatives must include structured digital learning platforms, content curation, and parent/teacher training on productive internet use. The positive internet effect seen in Higher Secondary-educated households (+0.116) demonstrates the template for success.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="rec-card">
    <span class="rec-tag">Medium Priority</span>
    <h4>4. Shift from "Study More" to "Study Smarter" Messaging</h4>
    <p>The absence of a positive correlation between study hours and outcomes (r = −0.022) means simply urging students to study more will not improve results. Invest in evidence-based study techniques: spaced repetition, active recall, and focused practice sessions. Quality of study time trumps quantity.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="rec-card">
    <span class="rec-tag">Strategic</span>
    <h4>5. Adopt District-Specific School Development Strategies</h4>
    <p>The public-private performance gap varies by +0.234 in Dhaka (public advantage) to −0.173 in Rajshahi (private advantage). A one-size-fits-all policy on school investment will not work. Each district needs tailored analysis of what makes its stronger school type effective, and those practices should be transferred to the weaker type locally.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="rec-card">
    <span class="rec-tag">Strategic</span>
    <h4>6. Investigate Unmeasured Factors Driving Performance</h4>
    <p>The weak predictive power of all measured variables (income, parent education, study hours, attendance) implies that critical drivers of HSC performance are not captured in this dataset. Future research should examine: teacher quality, curriculum delivery methods, exam preparation strategies, student mental health, and peer effects. Policy decisions should not rely solely on the variables measured here.</p>
  </div>
